{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/module.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/siteset_store.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/siteset_publication.cljc\n cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/module.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/siteset_store.cljc\n nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/siteset_publication.cljc\n cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yang.core.prelude\n     2\t  \"Emitter machinery shared by every language's runtime-profile prelude\n     3\t   (docs/design/yang.typescript.md §17, lift L1). A language supplies its\n     4\t   module's own namespace string, its uninit sentinel, its definition list\n     5\t   and its refusal namespace; everything here is a pure function of those.\"\n     6\t  (:require\n     7\t    [clojure.walk :as walk]\n     8\t    [yang.core.uast :as u]\n     9\t    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    10\t    [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(defn define\n    14\t  \"`(yin/def (quote key) form)` in prelude notation: the key quoted, so\n    15\t   `sexp->uast` reads it as the literal symbol Rule R requires.\"\n    16\t  [key form]\n    17\t  (list 'yin/def (list 'quote key) form))\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\t(defn strip\n    21\t  \"`sym` without the module's own namespace `ns-str`; any other value as it\n    22\t   is. Qualified internal store keys stay qualified.\"\n    23\t  [ns-str sym]\n    24\t  (if (and (symbol? sym) (= ns-str (namespace sym)))\n    25\t    (symbol (name sym))\n    26\t    sym))\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\t(defn strip-form\n    30\t  \"A prelude-notation form with every symbol `strip`ped: references,\n    31\t   definition keys and quoted keys alike. Keywords, strings and the\n    32\t   keys of literal maps are untouched.\"\n    33\t  [ns-str form]\n    34\t  (walk/postwalk (partial strip ns-str) form))\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(defn placeholder\n    38\t  \"The `[k uninit]` definition for runtime key `k`, so each read of it is\n    39\t   discharged by an unconditional module-level definition.\"\n    40\t  [k uninit]\n    41\t  [k uninit])\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn module-definitions\n    45\t  \"The module's definition list: a `uninit` placeholder for every runtime\n    46\t   key (stripped of `ns-str`), then every definition, stripped.\"\n    47\t  [ns-str uninit runtime-keys definitions]\n    48\t  (into (mapv (fn [k] (placeholder (strip ns-str k) uninit)) runtime-keys)\n    49\t        (map (fn [[k form]] [(strip ns-str k) (strip-form ns-str form)]))\n    50\t        definitions))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn wide-app\n    54\t  \"One application whose operands are `nodes` (in order), applied to a lambda\n    55\t   with one parameter per node that returns nil. Every operand runs on the main sequence before the one call, so the\n    56\t   linker's scanners discharge every body read of a sibling or runtime key;\n    57\t   a `then` chain would nest definitions in closure bodies.\"\n    58\t  [nodes]\n    59\t  (let [params (mapv (fn [i] (symbol (str \"%d\" i))) (range (count nodes)))]\n    60\t    (u/mark-tails (apply u/app (u/lam params (u/lit nil)) nodes))))\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn module-tree\n    64\t  \"The module tree for `defs` (`[key form]`, already stripped) followed by\n    65\t   the `trailing` UAST nodes.\"\n    66\t  [defs trailing]\n    67\t  (wide-app (into (mapv (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)\n    68\t                  trailing)))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defn exports\n    72\t  \"The module's exports: every key of `definitions` in namespace `ns-str`,\n    73\t   stripped.\"\n    74\t  [ns-str definitions]\n    75\t  (set (keep (fn [[k _]] (when (= ns-str (namespace k)) (strip ns-str k)))\n    76\t             definitions)))\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\t(defn free-names\n    80\t  \"Every variable name some occurrence of the map AST `ast` reads\n    81\t   unbound by an enclosing lambda.\"\n    82\t  [ast]\n    83\t  (letfn [(walk-node\n    84\t            [acc node bound]\n    85\t            (cond\n    86\t              (vector? node) (reduce (fn [a n] (walk-node a n bound)) acc node)\n    87\t              (not (map? node)) acc\n    88\t              :else\n    89\t              (case (:type node)\n    90\t                :variable (if (contains? bound (:name node))\n    91\t                            acc\n    92\t                            (conj acc (:name node)))\n    93\t                :literal acc\n    94\t                :lambda (walk-node acc (:body node) (into bound (:params node)))\n    95\t                (reduce (fn [a v] (walk-node a v bound)) acc (vals node)))))]\n    96\t    (walk-node #{} ast #{})))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t(defn defined-keys\n   100\t  \"Every literal key the map AST `ast` defines with `yin/def`, at any\n   101\t   depth.\"\n   102\t  [ast]\n   103\t  (set (keep (fn [node]\n   104\t               (when (and (map? node)\n   105\t                          (= :application (:type node))\n   106\t                          (= 'yin/def (get-in node [:operator :name])))\n   107\t                 (get-in node [:operands 0 :value])))\n   108\t             (tree-seq coll? seq ast))))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\t(defn free-name-vec\n   112\t  \"The names `ast` reads and does not define, sorted.\"\n   113\t  [ast]\n   114\t  (vec (sort-by str\n   115\t                (remove (conj (defined-keys ast) 'yin/def) (free-names ast)))))\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn- refusal\n   119\t  [refusal-ns kind data]\n   120\t  (merge {(keyword refusal-ns \"refusal\") (keyword refusal-ns kind)} data))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn host-declaration\n   124\t  \"`[n declaration]` for free name `n`: a primitive by its `vm/primitives`\n   125\t   profile, a host export `ns/name` by the profile address the registry's\n   126\t   host module `ns` publishes and its `:callable-effects` effect set; nil\n   127\t   when neither supplies it.\"\n   128\t  [registry n]\n   129\t  (if-let [profile (vm/profile-of vm/primitives n)]\n   130\t    [n profile]\n   131\t    (when-let [address (module/host-export-profile registry n)]\n   132\t      (let [entry (module/module-entry (:modules registry) (symbol (namespace n)))\n   133\t            f (get-in entry [:slice (symbol (name n))])]\n   134\t        [n {:yin.k/profile address,\n   135\t            :yin.k/effects (get (:callable-effects registry) f #{})}]))))\n   136\t\n   137\t\n   138\t(defn module-spec\n   139\t  \"`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`'s spec for module `module-name`.\n   140\t   `skip?` (n -> bool) marks free names a requirement already declares;\n   141\t   `requires` is the requirement map. A free name nothing supplies is\n   142\t   refused with `message` under `refusal-ns` before anything is published.\n   143\t   This namespace emits; it never publishes.\"\n   144\t  [{:keys [module-name ast exports requires free-names skip? registry message\n   145\t           refusal-ns]}]\n   146\t  {:name module-name,\n   147\t   :ast ast,\n   148\t   :exports exports,\n   149\t   :requires requires,\n   150\t   :primitives\n   151\t   (into {}\n   152\t         (keep (fn [n]\n   153\t                 (when-not (and skip? (skip? n))\n   154\t                   (or (host-declaration registry n)\n   155\t                       (throw (ex-info message\n   156\t                                       (refusal refusal-ns \"undeclared-free\"\n   157\t                                                {:name n})))))))\n   158\t         free-names)})\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\t(defn admit\n   162\t  \"Return `registry` when it supplies every one of `host-names`, else refuse\n   163\t   it before any program runs, naming each missing export in\n   164\t   `:<refusal-ns>/missing`.\"\n   165\t  [registry host-names message refusal-ns]\n   166\t  (let [missing (vec (sort-by str\n   167\t                              (remove #(some? (module/resolve-module\n   168\t                                                registry\n   169\t                                                (symbol (str (namespace %)\n   170\t                                                             \".\"\n   171\t                                                             (name %)))))\n   172\t                                      host-names)))]\n   173\t    (when (seq missing)\n   174\t      (throw (ex-info message\n   175\t                      (refusal refusal-ns \"host-names\"\n   176\t                               {(keyword refusal-ns \"missing\") missing}))))\n   177\t    registry))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defn balanced-definitions->uast\n   181\t  \"Balanced definition-to-UAST emission: each [key form] pair maps to\n   182\t   u/def! and the vector splits at its midpoint, halving sequential depth\n   183\t   versus the linear u/seq-nodes chain. The tree shape is content-addressed:\n   184\t   empty input answers nil, and callers choose balanced versus linear\n   185\t   deliberately - never migrate between the two for style.\"\n   186\t  [defs]\n   187\t  (letfn [(sequence-tree [nodes]\n   188\t            (if (<= (count nodes) 1)\n   189\t              (first nodes)\n   190\t              (let [middle (quot (count nodes) 2)]\n   191\t                (u/then (sequence-tree (subvec nodes 0 middle))\n   192\t                        (sequence-tree (subvec nodes middle))))))]\n   193\t    (sequence-tree (mapv (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs))))\n   194\t\n   195\t\n   196\t(defn definitions->uast\n   197\t  [defs]\n   198\t  (u/seq-nodes (map (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)))\n     1\t(ns yang.core.module\n     2\t  \"Module emitter machinery shared by every language frontend\n     3\t   (docs/design/yang.typescript.md §17, lift L3): the linker module naming\n     4\t   rule, the `require` and wide-application tree nodes, free-name\n     5\t   declaration over a host registry, and the `module-spec` and SCC bundle\n     6\t   shapes `yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!` takes. A language supplies\n     7\t   its prefix, its runtime module and its module AST; nothing here knows a\n     8\t   source language.\"\n     9\t  (:require\n    10\t    [clojure.string :as str]\n    11\t    [yang.core.prelude :as prelude]\n    12\t    [yang.core.uast :as u]\n    13\t    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    14\t    [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    15\t\n    16\t\n    17\t(defn module-symbol\n    18\t  \"The linker module name of source module `name` under language `prefix`\n    19\t   (\\\"py\\\", \\\"php\\\", \\\"js\\\"): `<prefix>m.` then the name with each dot a `$`,\n    20\t   so no linker module name is a dotted prefix of another.\"\n    21\t  [prefix name]\n    22\t  (symbol (str prefix \"m.\" (str/replace name \".\" \"$\"))))\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn require-node\n    26\t  \"(require 'm) as a tree node.\"\n    27\t  [m]\n    28\t  (u/sexp->uast (list 'require (list 'quote m))))\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t(defn module-tree\n    32\t  \"A wide application: all definitions remain at module level.\"\n    33\t  [operands]\n    34\t  (prelude/wide-app operands))\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(defn declare-free-name\n    38\t  \"The `:primitives` entry free name `n` declares over `registry`, or nil\n    39\t   when `covered` (the requirement-pinned module names) covers it. A name\n    40\t   no `vm/primitives` profile or host export supplies throws, with\n    41\t   `refusal` under `refusal-key` in its data.\"\n    42\t  [refusal-key refusal registry covered n]\n    43\t  (if (and (symbol? n) (some? (namespace n))\n    44\t           (contains? covered (symbol (namespace n))))\n    45\t    nil\n    46\t    (if-let [profile (vm/profile-of vm/primitives n)]\n    47\t      [n profile]\n    48\t      (if-let [address (module/host-export-profile registry n)]\n    49\t        (let [entry (module/module-entry (:modules registry)\n    50\t                                         (symbol (namespace n)))\n    51\t              f (get-in entry [:slice (symbol (name n))])]\n    52\t          [n {:yin.k/profile address,\n    53\t              :yin.k/effects\n    54\t              (get (:callable-effects registry) f #{})}])\n    55\t        (throw (ex-info \"the module reads an undeclared name\"\n    56\t                        {refusal-key refusal, :name n}))))))\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\t(defn free-primitives\n    60\t  \"The `:primitives` map declaring every free name of `ast` over `registry`.\"\n    61\t  [refusal-key refusal registry covered ast]\n    62\t  (let [free (remove (conj (prelude/defined-keys ast) 'yin/def)\n    63\t                     (prelude/free-names ast))]\n    64\t    (into {}\n    65\t          (keep (partial declare-free-name refusal-key refusal registry covered))\n    66\t          (sort-by str free))))\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\t(defn module-spec\n    70\t  \"`publish-module!`'s spec for module `name`: `ast` with `exports`, requiring\n    71\t   the runtime module `runtime-name` at `runtime-address` and each of `deps`\n    72\t   ({module-symbol address}). A free name under a required module is covered\n    73\t   by it; any other is declared as `declare-free-name` does.\"\n    74\t  [refusal-key refusal registry\n    75\t   {:keys [name ast exports runtime-name runtime-address deps]}]\n    76\t  (let [requires (into {runtime-name runtime-address} deps)]\n    77\t    {:name name,\n    78\t     :ast ast,\n    79\t     :exports exports,\n    80\t     :requires requires,\n    81\t     :primitives (free-primitives refusal-key refusal registry\n    82\t                                  (set (keys requires)) ast)}))\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\t(defn alias-spec\n    86\t  \"A thin member alias of the SCC bundle `bundle-name`: it requires the\n    87\t   bundle and defines `spec` and `body` as the bundle's `spec-key` and\n    88\t   `body-key` exports.\"\n    89\t  [name exports require-profile bundle-name spec-key body-key]\n    90\t  {:name name,\n    91\t   :exports exports,\n    92\t   :primitives {'require require-profile},\n    93\t   :ast (module-tree\n    94\t          [(require-node bundle-name)\n    95\t           (u/def! 'spec (u/v (symbol (str bundle-name) (str spec-key))))\n    96\t           (u/def! 'body (u/v (symbol (str bundle-name) (str body-key))))])})\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t(defn scc-module-specs\n   100\t  \"The SCC publication shape: the `bundle` spec first, then the member alias\n   101\t   specs (each `alias-spec`'s output) in member order.\"\n   102\t  [bundle aliases]\n   103\t  (into [bundle] aliases))\n     1\t(ns yang.core.siteset-store\n     2\t  \"Shared tree loading and buffered store operations.\"\n     3\t  (:require [yin.vm :as vm]))\n     4\t\n     5\t\n     6\t(defn load-tree\n     7\t  \"The canonical row set `{:root :rows}` of the tree at `root` in `handle`,\n     8\t   every row fetched by its address, depth-first: `{:status :missing\n     9\t   :address a}` for the first row the handle does not hold, nil when a row\n    10\t   is not its address or no tree row.\"\n    11\t  [read-at absent? handle root]\n    12\t  (loop [todo [root]\n    13\t         rows {}]\n    14\t    (if-let [id (peek todo)]\n    15\t      (if (contains? rows id)\n    16\t        (recur (pop todo) rows)\n    17\t        (let [body (read-at handle id)\n    18\t              slots (when (vector? body)\n    19\t                      (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (first body)))]\n    20\t          (cond\n    21\t            (absent? body) {:status :missing, :address id}\n    22\t            slots (recur (into (pop todo)\n    23\t                               (mapcat (fn [[_ kind] v]\n    24\t                                         (case kind\n    25\t                                           :node [v]\n    26\t                                           :nodes v\n    27\t                                           nil))\n    28\t                                       slots\n    29\t                                       (rest body)))\n    30\t                         (assoc rows id (into [id] body)))\n    31\t            :else nil)))\n    32\t      {:root root, :rows rows})))\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t(defn overlay\n    36\t  \"A write-buffering handle over `base`: reads see the buffer first, then\n    37\t   `base`; puts land in the buffer only, in put order. `flush!` writes the\n    38\t   buffer to `base`; dropping the overlay writes nothing.\"\n    39\t  [base]\n    40\t  (let [buf (atom {:bytes {}, :order []})\n    41\t        sentinel #?(:cljs (js-obj)\n    42\t                    :default (Object.))]\n    43\t    {:handle (assoc base\n    44\t                    :get-bytes-fn (fn [address not-found]\n    45\t                                    (let [bs (get-in @buf [:bytes address])]\n    46\t                                      (if (nil? bs)\n    47\t                                        ((:get-bytes-fn base) address not-found)\n    48\t                                        bs)))\n    49\t                    :put-bytes-fn (fn [address bs]\n    50\t                                    (if (or (contains? (:bytes @buf) address)\n    51\t                                            (let [existing ((:get-bytes-fn base)\n    52\t                                                            address\n    53\t                                                            sentinel)]\n    54\t                                              (and (some? existing)\n    55\t                                                   (not (identical? sentinel\n    56\t                                                                    existing)))))\n    57\t                                      :present\n    58\t                                      (do (swap! buf (fn [b]\n    59\t                                                       (-> b\n    60\t                                                           (assoc-in [:bytes address]\n    61\t                                                                     bs)\n    62\t                                                           (update :order conj\n    63\t                                                                   address))))\n    64\t                                          :inserted))))\n    65\t     :flush! (fn []\n    66\t               (let [{:keys [bytes order]} @buf]\n    67\t                 (doseq [a order] ((:put-bytes-fn base) a (get bytes a)))))}))\n     1\t(ns yang.core.siteset-publication\n     2\t  \"Shared signed site publication operations.\"\n     3\t  (:require [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\n     4\t\n     5\t\n     6\t(defn signed-entry\n     7\t  \"Sign the association between a published manifest and its site record.\"\n     8\t  [key manifest record-address]\n     9\t  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\n    10\t             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\n    11\t             :yang.site/record record-address,\n    12\t             :yin.module/asserted-by (sign/principal (:public key))}]\n    13\t    {:envelope env, :proof (sign/sign-envelope (:seed key) env)}))\n    14\t\n    15\t\n    16\t(defn entry-datoms\n    17\t  \"Index signed site publications alongside name assertions.\"\n    18\t  [signed]\n    19\t  (vec (mapcat (fn [e {:keys [envelope proof]}]\n    20\t                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\n    21\t                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\n    22\t               (range) signed)))\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn signed-entries\n    26\t  \"Sign all site publications returned by unit publication, including aliases.\"\n    27\t  [publisher sites addresses bundles]\n    28\t  (mapv (fn [[k s]]\n    29\t          (signed-entry publisher (or (get addresses k) (get bundles k))\n    30\t                        (:record-address s)))\n    31\t        (sort-by (comp str key) sites)))\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refact