{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,48p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\n sed -n '1,48p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc\n sed -n '1,42p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\n sed -n '1,35p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\n rg -n 'C5|export-tree|zero|out: 2|\\\\+8' collab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md collab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"(ns ^{:clj-kondo/config '{:lint-as {yang.php.antlr.quoted/defquoted clojure.core/def}}}\n yang.php.antlr.prelude\n  \"The PHP runtime profile, written as Universal AST\n   (docs/design/yang.php.md sections 3.5, 7, 9). PHP semantics for operators,\n   juggling, comparison, output and errors live here as guest code, not in\n   Clojure: the lowering only emits applications of these names.\n\n   Value encoding (P1):\n     int             host integer in [-2^63, 2^63-1] (a BigInt carrier on JS\n                     past 2^53); every operator computes the exact result\n                     through the `integer` module and demotes to float when\n                     it leaves the range\n     float           untagged Jing float64 content (`data/float64`), the\n                     shared ruling of yang.typescript.md section 2; the kind\n                     test is `php/kind`, never a second tag. The prelude\n                     holds no integral float literal (JS reads 1.0 as 1):\n                     floats are built with `data/float64` or computed, and\n                     host arithmetic only ever sees `data/float-value`\n     string          a host string whose every char is one byte (a latin-1\n                     carrier); the lowering resolves literal text to bytes\n     bool            host true / false\n     null            :php/null\n     array           {:php/type :array :index {key value} :order [key ...]\n                     :next-index n}: a persistent ordered map; keys are\n                     ints or strings (`php/array-key`); `:next-index` is\n                     absent until the first integer key is written\n     error object    {:php/type :obj :class name :ord n :props {\\\"message\\\"\n                     \\\"code\\\" \\\"previous\\\"}}\n     function        a cell {:php/type :function :name s :code (fn [args])\n                             :yang.call/entry (fn [invocation])}\n\n   Runtime state is allocated by `php/init!`, once per task, under the slot\n   `php.rt/state` (the literal `:php/uninit` until `php/init!` flips it to\n   `:php/ready` as its last write):\n     php.rt/ctx            the dynamic context {:handlers :depth :base :frame}\n     php.rt/out            a cell holding the buffer stack, a vector of\n                           buffers, each a vector of byte-string chunks;\n                           `php/echo` conjs onto the last buffer, the\n                           bottom buffer is never popped\n     php.rt/globals        a cell holding the plain array of section 10.1\n                           (`$GLOBALS`), `(php/empty-array)` initially\n     php.rt/functions      a cell holding a map of lower-cased name to\n                           function cell; builtins are pre-seeded\n     php.rt/classes        a cell holding a map of class name to class record\n                           (the Throwable hierarchy, data only)\n     php.rt/next-ord       a cell, the per-task object ordinal\n     php.rt/next-cell      a cell, the per-task counted-cell id (`:id`)\n     php.rt/request        a cell holding the explicit request record\n(ns ^{:clj-kondo/config '{:lint-as {yang.python.antlr.prelude/defquoted clojure.core/def}}}\n yang.python.antlr.prelude\n  \"The Python runtime profile, written as Universal AST\n   (docs/design/yang.antlr.md §8.1, §8.2, §9.3). Python semantics for\n   operators, truthiness, equality, objects, exceptions and escapes live\n   here as guest code, not in Clojure: the lowering only emits applications\n   of these names.\n\n   Value encoding:\n     int             host integer (untagged)\n     float           {:py/float x}: tagged on every host, because JS cannot\n                     tell 2 from 2.0 (owner decision 3); x is float64\n                     content (`data/float64`), never a bare host number,\n                     so 2.0 has one address on every host. Arithmetic\n                     unwraps with `data/float-value` and computes on bare\n                     host numbers. The prelude holds no integral float\n                     literal (JS reads 1.0 as 1), and no float literal\n                     reaches host arithmetic except through\n                     `data/float-value`, which also bridges a float64\n                     carrier decoded from a row back to a host number:\n                     1.0 is written (data/float-value 1)\n     bool            host true / false (numeric as 1 / 0)\n     None            :py/None\n     str             {:py/str \\\"...\\\"}\n     list            a cell holding {:py/type :list :items [...]}\n     dict            a cell holding {:py/type :dict :index {nkey slot}\n                     :keys [...] :vals [...]}: insertion order is the keys\n                     vector, never a host map's order; keys are normalized\n                     so 1, 1.0 and True are one key (see `py/key`)\n     set             a cell holding {:py/type :set :index {nkey slot}\n                     :keys [...]}: insertion order, keys normalized as for\n                     dicts\n     function        a cell holding {:py/type :function :name s :spec spec\n                     :defaults [...] :kwdefaults [[name v] ...]\n                     :code (fn [args] ...) :yang.call/entry\n                     (fn [invocation] ...)}; identity is the cell ref\n     class           a cell holding {:py/type :class :name s :base cls\n                     :attrs {name value}}\n     instance        a cell holding {:py/type :instance :class cls\n                     :attrs {name value}}\n     generator       a cell holding {:py/type :generator :name s :state st}\n                     and the slots of its state (see `py/gen-switch`)\n     iterator        a cell holding {:py/type :iterator :src s :i n}: what\n                     iter() makes of a sequence, advancing `py/iter-at` over\n                     src once per next; src None once exhausted\n     bound method    {:py/type :method :self obj :fn function}\n     tuple           {:py/type :tuple :items [...]}: a value; hashable when\n                     its elements are\n(ns yin.vm\n  \"Canonical data model (Datoms), schema, and primitives for the Yin Abstract\n   Machine on DaoStream v2.\n\n   The shared kernel of `yin.vm` carries over unchanged. What changes is the\n   stream wiring:\n\n   - **The host supplies streams.** v1 hardcoded a ring buffer descriptor and\n     called `ds/open!`. Here a composition hands the interpreter a\n     `:make-stream` constructor exactly as it hands it `:primitives`. There is\n     no default, because a default would smuggle the hardcoded transport back\n     in: absent a supplied constructor, `:stream/make` is unsupported and says\n     so.\n   - **Program observation lives outside the VM.** The program stream handle,\n     cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`; the VM never\n     polls a program stream and no longer accepts `:in-stream` at\n     construction. Language-level stream effects and FFI still operate their\n     own streams and cursors inside the VM.\n   - **Cursors are opaque.** The VM's internal cursor representation is\n     `{:stream-id id :cursor <opaque>}`. There is no position and no seek.\n   - **Construction is all-or-nothing.** Creating the FFI pair and minting its\n     cursors are stream operations with their own outcomes. Any non-`ok`\n     outcome fails construction; a VM cannot be half-built.\n   - **`call-in-cursor-key` is dropped.** v1 wrote it and nothing read it.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.host.integer :as integer-host]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.vm.effect :as effect]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Protocols\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Common VM interfaces implemented by all execution engines.\n\n(defprotocol IVM\n(ns yin.vm.module\n  \"Module system for Yin VM v2.\n\n   The v1 module system kept two `defonce` atoms and mutated them at load\n   time. A registry is a value here: an ordinary map carried in VM state and\n   supplied by the composition that wires the VM.\n\n     (-> (empty-registry)\n         (register-host-module 'my.lib {'foo (fn [x] x)} profiles)\n         (register-stream-module))\n\n   Nothing in this namespace runs at load time, and nothing registers itself.\n   A composition that wants Yin source to reach `(stream/...)` registers the\n   stream module explicitly and supplies `:make-stream` beside it.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.jing :as jing]\n    [dao.stream :as stream]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.effect :as effect]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Registry values\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn empty-registry\n  \"An empty module registry value.\"\n  []\n  {:modules {}, :effect-handlers {}})\n\n\n(defn- symbol->path\n  \"Split a dotted symbol into a get-in path of symbols.\n   'yin.io.file-input-stream → ['yin 'io 'file-input-stream]\"\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:27:- **C5 (`own-lexer!`/`own-parser!` in `cst.clj`; `lexer-span`/`export-tree` in `yang.antlr.export`):**\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:28:  - **High cohesion & structural clustering — IMPROVED (with required visibility demotion in §4):** `own-lexer!` and `own-parser!` are private (`defn-`) in [cst.clj](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj), adding 0 public API while aligning JVM with `.cljs` and `.cljd`. Lifting `lexer-span` and `export-tree` into [yang.antlr.export](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/antlr/export.cljc) centralizes host-free offset and packet assembly across all 4 hosts (`clj`, `cljs`, `cljd`, `jolt`).\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:45:   - Because all 7 callers of `module-spec`/`admit` (`yang.core.call`, `clojure/prelude`, `php/antlr/prelude`, `python/antlr/prelude`, `typescript/antlr/prelude`, `python/antlr/safepoint`, `typescript/antlr/safepoint`) still require `yang.core.prelude` for `module-definitions`, `module-tree`, `exports`, and `free-name-vec`, extracting `yang.core.prelude-admission` adds `+1` outgoing `:require` edge to each of those 7 namespaces and to `yang.core.module` (+8 architectural edges to a stable `out: 2` leaf, in exchange for decoupling `yang.core.prelude` from `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.module`).\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:61:1. **In C5 (`yang.antlr.export`), demote the 3 subsumed public functions (`offset-at`, `export-nodes`, `result`) to `defn-` in the same pass:**\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:63:   - Adding `lexer-span` and `export-tree` without demoting the functions they replace would grow `yang.antlr.export` from **7 → 9 public functions**, violating bounded complexity.\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:64:   - Once [cst.clj](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj), [cst.cljs](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs), [cst.cljd](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/yang/antlr/cst.cljd), and [cst.jolt](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt) call `lexer-span` and `export-tree`, `offset-at`, `export-nodes`, and `result` have **zero external callers** in `src/` or `test/`. Marking those 3 functions `defn-` in C5 **shrinks** `yang.antlr.export`'s public surface from **7 → 6** (`byte-offsets`, `token-span`, `lexer-span`, `syntax-error`, `export-tree`, `failure-kind`) instead of growing it to 9.\ncollab/exception-track/1795022027297-architect-dedup-round2-malleability-conformance.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:70:CONFORMANCE VERDICT: ALIGNS WITH AMENDMENTS — Merge C2 and the `core.prelude-admission` split into one atomic unit to avoid re-wiring `yang.core.module` twice, drop the dead 4-arg `load-tree` arity in C1, and mark the 3 subsumed functions (`offset-at`, `export-nodes`, `result`) `defn-` in C5 so `yang.antlr.export` shrinks from 7 to 6 public functions instead of growing to 9.\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:306:### Cluster C5: CST Host Exporters (`src/{clj,cljs,cljd,jolt}/yang/antlr/cst.*`) and `yang.antlr.export`\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:333:  2. Add `lexer-span` (`[offsets start stop] [(offset-at offsets start) (offset-at offsets stop)]`) and `export-tree` (`[profile unit source errors ops-fn tree]`) to [yang.antlr.export](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/antlr/export.cljc).\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:424:| **5** | **C5** | Factor `own-lexer!` and `own-parser!` in [src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj#L132-L148) (matching `.cljs`/`.cljd`) and lift `lexer-span` / `export-tree` into [yang.antlr.export](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/antlr/export.cljc). | **25–30 lines** | High | Removes internal simulator-setup duplication in `cst.clj` and centralizes error-span/export orchestration without tangling host interop in reader conditionals. |\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:459:Caller analysis across `src/` and `test/` shows **6 public functions with zero external callers** that violate the [malleability.md](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/malleability.md#L78-L83) rule (*\"Mark a function private (`defn-`) if it is called only from within its own namespace\"*):\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:472:  1. **Pure UAST/S-exp Prelude Emission & AST Free-Name Scanning** (`define`, `strip`, `module-definitions`, `wide-app`, `module-tree`, `exports`, `free-names`, `defined-keys`, `free-name-vec`, `balanced-definitions->uast`, `definitions->uast`): depends **only** on `clojure.walk` and `yang.core.uast` (`out: 2`).\ncollab/exception-track/1795020669393-architect-yang-dedup-round2-audit.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md:481:  2. The entire file is 111 lines with low outgoing coupling (`out: 2` — `yang.antlr.packet` and `yang.core.uast`).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
